      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fry the birista onion. Slice 1 onion very thin. Fry in 3 tablespoons of oil over medium heat, stirring, for 20 minutes until deep golden. Drain on paper and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In the same oil, fry cinnamon, cardamom, cloves, and bay leaves for 1 minute. Add the 2 remaining chopped onions. Cook 12 minutes until golden."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add garlic, ginger, turmeric, chilli powder, and coriander. Stir 2 minutes. Add lamb, yoghurt, and salt. Brown the lamb for 10 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add 1.5 litres water. Bring to a boil, skim foam, then cover and simmer 90 minutes until the lamb is falling off the bone."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove bones. Add soaked semolina and both lentils. Stir and cook uncovered 45 minutes over low heat, stirring every 10 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Partially blend with a hand blender to a rough porridge. Stir in ghee and garam masala. Taste and adjust salt. Serve topped with birista, mint, and lime."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Haleem is sticking to the bottom of the pot",
              "cause": "Not enough stirring at the thickening stage",
              "fix": "Add 100 ml hot water, stir vigorously, and reduce heat to the lowest setting."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Texture is too smooth",
              "cause": "Over-blended",
              "fix": "Haleem should be rough and fibrous. Use a wooden spoon or masher instead of a blender."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Haleem has roots in the Persian dish harees and was brought to Hyderabad by the Nizams. Over centuries it merged with South Indian spicing to become a distinctly Hyderabadi dish. It is particularly associated with Ramadan, when street vendors in the Old City cook it in enormous copper pots. In 2010 it received a Geographical Indication tag, the first meat product in India to do so. It is eaten for iftar and at late-night wedding suppers."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
